Language: en

00:00:00.400 --> 00:00:05.510 align:start position:0%
 
[Music]  what do you think my mother  in istanbul turkey has in common with  the first lady of the united states  let me give you a tip what they have in  common is not the fancy house  i grew up in a modest home with lovely  parents  when my mother got home from work every  day she had a four hour second shift  a shift made up of grocery shopping  cooking cleaning laundry  ironing helping me with homework  preparing me for school  my mother worked a full-time job and on  top of that when she got home she  carried the burden of  all of this traditional household duties  duties that many women tend to shoulder  when they're in relationships with male  partners  just as my mother is expected to perform  domestic  work unpaid the first lady is expected  to perform community work  on an unpaid basis my mother and the  first lady are both  expected to perform these duties by  default  in order to advance the well-being of  their husbands  of their communities and of their  families  look we expect the first lady to perform  a demanding full-time job  for free she conducts speaking  engagements  event planning household management  strategic advising  a cool job that money magazine expects  would pay 287  000 a year in washington d.c  yes the first lady is not the average  woman  but she still reflects society's  expectations of the average woman  the expectation of free labor for women  is so ingrained in our  collective psyche that even the first  lady is not  immune to it but here's a plot twist  we just elected our first second spouse  so what happens when mr kamala harris  becomes the expectation  becomes the norm and not just the  exception  what happens when more women are in  charge and what happens if their  partners  are men how many of those men are going  to agree to quit  their jobs and hang up christmas  decorations  first spouses have been women for all of  history  and sadly society has become complacent  in the expectation of free labor  from women look  men are four times more likely to  negotiate for a raise than women  if the first spouses had been man all  along  i have a feeling that they would have  revolted and asked for a raise  a long time ago  so what if we made it  optional and we gave the first spouse  the option to opt in  to perform the duties of the office as  opposed to just expecting their service  as an extension of their spouse  what would happen if we compensated them  when they opt-in  what would happen if we made an example  at the very top  that domestic and community work is  worthy  of recognition what would that mean  for women around the country  the first lady is very important because  she's symbolic even though  she has privileges that come as a result  of her office  so let's discuss who carries the real  burden  why this is the time to talk about this  and what we can do to resolve the issue  the real burden of unpaid work falls on  underprivileged women lower income women  and especially  women of color let's take a step back  and look at the big picture first the  federal reserve estimates  that women's contribution to the u.s  economy  annually in form of domestic work is  about 1.2  trillion dollars that is the size  of the new york economy that number for  women globally was 11 trillion dollars  the sizes of japanese german and indian  economies  combined this work is so critical  that in 1975 icelandic women just quit  taking care of their homes and their  children for one day  it is reported that the whole nation  came to a standstill  and let's talk about women of color  because women of color  don't just carry the burden of unpaid  domestic work  they also carry a whole history of  injustice  and the consequences of it nina banks  associate professor at bucknell  university researched  how community activism is yet another  form  of unpaid labor that is conducted by  black women  and other marginalized women of color  this work has this grassroots movements  have provided  food security and housing security to  their communities for decades  but they have been improperly  categorized as political work  when really they should be categorized  as labor  labor that fills in the gaps where  public  and private institutions have failed  marginalized communities  but why now you might ask why is this  the time to take  action because thanks to the pandemic  and failed systems women are reaching a  breaking point  they're losing their jobs and they're  experiencing the increased burden of  housework and child care  disproportionately according to new york  times 5  million women have lost their jobs  during the pandemic  over 30 percent of those women ages 25  to 44  said that child care was the reason for  that unemployment  we are back to 1980s levels of women's  activity in the workforce  and guess what black and brown women  have been hit  absolutely the hardest  let's not forget one more thing women  have also  care have also been carrying the  increased mental health burden of the  pandemic  disproportionately about 70 percent of  mothers say that they  experience adverse health effects due to  the stress of the pandemic  compared to about 50 of fathers  but also let's remember that the  pandemic did not  create this crisis the pandemic just  highlighted  the inequalities in our broken systems  so you might be thinking what can we do  about it  as individuals as employers and as  government officials  let's start as individuals firstly we  can start by challenging our own biases  because it's only when we create  individual awareness  that we can challenge greater systems  and challenge the unequal ways in which  our communities  view gender norms as a place to start  we can challenge the prevalent norm that  in many communities and cultures  mothers are the ones who are expected to  step into the role of the primary parent  we can make it okay for fathers to be  full-time fathers  and if they choose to do so we can  remove any stigma  and relieve them of the stigma  surrounding that decision  another thing we can do is to decouple  housework  from gender norms there's actually a lot  of studies that have shown  that housework is actually split more  equitably in same-sex  relationships we can change our mindsets  such that heterosexual couples also sit  down to have those  difficult conversations to decide how to  divide the labor equitably  without just defaulting on traditional  gender roles  next what can we do as government  officers  well mandating table stakes maternally  would be a good place to start  let me just recount this shocking but  still true statistic  that the united states is the only  developed country  that doesn't provide national statutory  paid  maternal leave but  that's not even enough fathers should  also  be able to take statutory parental leave  this will not only help fathers not only  help mothers  in relationships heterosexual  relationships but it will also help gay  fathers take the time that they need and  deserve  to be able to take care of their  children  another thing that the governments can  do is to really prioritize  investments in preschool programs  especially in lower income communities  and let's remember that over 70 percent  of all seats in the congress  and all statewide elected offices are  occupied by  men change will not happen  until male allies in power take these  ideas  and put them into legislation  and lastly what can we do as employers  since this is the business school as  employers  we can provide parental leave even when  men not mandated by the government  we can also make sure that our employees  take the leave  and we can eliminate any cultural stigma  and relieve them  of any penalization that comes from  their temporary absence  beyond that we can also take all of our  learnings from the pandemic  and apply them after the pandemic we can  make flexible work  and remove work accommodations available  to families  especially especially the families who  are taking care of children  and the elderly and let's remember  that today in 2021 more than 90 percent  of ceos or fortune 500 companies are  men so male allies  at this top business school ready to  take over  boardrooms and executive offices in not  too distant future  you have the power to change  organizations  you have the power to set in place  policies  that counteract the status quo  and all of that starts when we start  noticing the biases in our minds  in our hearts and in our social norms  and let's not forget to make an example  a symbolic example at the very top  let's pay the first lady thank you  [Applause]  [Music]

